From ace991ff7b757b9526610556e04dfb594cf778d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rodney Pantonial <rodneypantonial@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 04:44:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Load-up checks: spread the inventory check over frames too

The inventory check was the largest step left in one frame (about three quarters of the load-up stall in our profiles): it looks up the localized name of every object in the world and reads the catalog name of every inventory item. It now uses the shared helper's time-based overload with kScanSleepInterval, so it sleeps between objects and between items of large inventories, and it skips the name lookup for objects without an inventory. NRaas/Helpers/Inventories.cs is the same file as in the Overwatch pull request #45, so the two merge cleanly in either order; the helper keeps the old behaviour for its other callers.

Same results: the helper re-checks after each sleep what the game may have changed (stacks, items, destroyed objects) and leaves items added meanwhile for the next check, as the one-frame check would have run before they came. Because the load-up checks can now sleep before the dereference pass, a world quit during those sleeps skips the remaining inventory and controller work and goes to the end, as a quit during the dereference pass already does. Between controller items the sleep now comes before the world-quit check, so a quit during it is seen before the next item.

Assisted with AI
